  its name in `service.yaml` and `migrate-job.yaml`. Each secret is a
  secret of its own, with the key (Knative) or the version (Cloud Run)
  `latest`. Run the migrations before each deployment of a new image.
  On a cluster with Knative Serving:

  ```sh
{% for env in secrets %}
  kubectl create secret generic {{ app.name }}-{{ env | lower | replace("_", "-") }} --from-literal=latest='<{{ env }}>'
{% endfor %}
  kubectl delete job {{ app.name }}-migrate --ignore-not-found
  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/migrate-job.yaml
  kubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/{{ app.name }}-migrate --timeout=10m
  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/service.yaml
  ```

  On Cloud Run, a Cloud Run job runs the migrations. The database must
  accept connections from Cloud Run, and the service account of the
  service and of the job must have access to the secrets:

  ```sh
{% for env in secrets %}
  printf %s '<{{ env }}>' | gcloud secrets create {{ app.name }}-{{ env | lower | replace("_", "-") }} --data-file=-
{% endfor %}
  gcloud run jobs deploy {{ app.name }}-migrate --image {{ image }} \
    --command alembic --args upgrade,head \
    --set-secrets DATABASE_URL={{ app.name }}-database-url:latest --execute-now --wait
  gcloud run services replace deploy/knative/service.yaml
  ```

  A new Cloud Run service accepts only requests with a Google sign-in.
  For a public API, run `gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding
  {{ app.name }}-api --member=allUsers --role=roles/run.invoker`.
